  "slug": "edge-non-hermitian-4a89",
  "summary": "Four preprints show how gain, loss, and coupling asymmetry control non-Hermitian photonic and spectral features, from BIC merging to exceptional-point splitting.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is control of non-Hermitian spectral structure through gain-loss balance and coupling asymmetry. One preprint uses a four-mode model of C2-symmetric photonic-crystal slabs, where coupling sign governs BIC merging, Q~k^-6 scaling, and flatband formation. Another finds that T-symmetry breaking induced by gain and loss is forbidden in time-dependent T-symmetric systems, even for random gain-loss distributions. A third introduces pseudo-altermagnetism, in which balanced gain and loss mimic altermagnetic spin splitting with movable nodal structures. A fourth shows that tunable nonreciprocal coupling near a second-order polarization exceptional point produces state-selective eigenstate splitting. What is new is the set of specific control knobs and constraints, not a single unified result; the summaries do not tie the items to edge phenomena in particular.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
